-- D6 state foundation, tier B continued: Attorney General + Secretary of State +
-- Treasurer, the states where each is a popularly ELECTED office. Same shape as
-- migrations 061 (President/VP) and 063 (Governor/Lt.Governor) -- small hand-verified
-- data, not an ingester, since no unified API covers statewide executive offices and
-- this project's discipline is to never guess a living person's office from
-- training-data memory.
--
-- Unlike Governor/Lt.Governor, these three offices are genuinely NOT universal or
-- uniformly elected -- verified live 2026-08-12 against Ballotpedia's per-office pages
-- and current-officeholder rosters (raw HTML extracted and parsed directly, same
-- discipline as every other large table pulled this session, after an earlier
-- AI-summarized fetch attempt this session had already hallucinated a real fact):
--
--  - Attorney General exists as an office in all 50 states, but is only POPULARLY
--    ELECTED in 43: Maine's is chosen by the legislature, Tennessee's by the state
--    supreme court, and Alaska/Hawaii/New Hampshire/New Jersey/Wyoming's by the
--    governor. Confirmed via Ballotpedia's own page text (43-state figure, Maine +
--    Tennessee named explicitly) plus a corroborating search for the specific
--    "remaining five" governor-appointed states.
--  - Secretary of State does not exist AT ALL in Alaska, Hawaii, or Utah (confirmed in
--    Ballotpedia's page text: "a state-level position in 47 of the 50 states"). Of the
--    47 that have it, 35 are elected and 12 are appointed (governor: Delaware, Florida,
--    Maryland, New Jersey, New York, Oklahoma, Pennsylvania, Texas, Virginia;
--    legislature: Maine, New Hampshire, Tennessee) -- this state list is sourced from a
--    secondary aggregation (not Ballotpedia's own primary chart, which loads its
--    selection-method breakdown via an embedded Datawrapper chart this migration
--    couldn't extract), cross-checked against the primary page's own arithmetic (35+12
--    =47) and against Maryland's own officeholder data (the current Secretary's term
--    start lines up with a cabinet appointment date, not an election cycle) -- flagged
--    as the lowest-confidence classification in this migration, worth a follow-up
--    verification pass.
--  - Treasurer does not exist in Texas or New York (a Comptroller does the job in both
--    -- already known from earlier research this project did). Of the 48 states that
--    have it, 36 are elected and 12 are appointed. This state list, unlike Secretary of
--    State's, comes directly from the PRIMARY source data: every state whose current
--    Treasurer-equivalent officeholder is tagged "Nonpartisan" on Ballotpedia's own
--    roster (Alaska, Georgia, Hawaii, Maine, Maryland, Michigan, Minnesota, Montana, New
--    Hampshire, New Jersey, Tennessee, Virginia) -- exactly 12 matches, exactly matching
--    the page's stated "appointed in 12." Several of these aren't even titled
--    "Treasurer" in their own state (Alaska's is the Commissioner of the Department of
--    Revenue, Hawaii's the Director of Finance, Minnesota's the Commissioner of
--    Management and Budget, Montana's the Director of the Department of Revenue) --
--    real agency heads, not a popularly-elected constitutional office, so excluded the
--    same way every other non-elected seat is excluded from this project's ballot data.
--
-- Term length is 4 years for every row except Vermont (2 years, matching its Governor --
-- VT runs its entire statewide ticket on a short cycle), verified via a targeted search
-- rather than assumed uniform, but NOT independently re-checked state-by-state the way
-- the state legislature's NCSL table was -- disclosed as the one gap in this migration's
-- research depth, not silently assumed complete.
--
-- A few source rows gave only a bare year ("1995", "2011", "2019") instead of a full
-- date for very long-tenured incumbents -- term_start for those defaults to January 1 of
-- that year, flagged in each row's own bio text rather than silently treated as exact.



